  "Question": "\\[ \\sqrt{\\frac{8^{10}+4^{10}}{8^4+4^{11}}}= \\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
    "B": "\\(16\\)",
    "C": "\\(32\\)",
    "D": "\\(12^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\)",
    "E": "\\(512.5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Since \\(8=2^3\\) and \\(4=2^2\\), writing both bases as powers of \\(2\\) helps reveal common factors in the two sums. The numerator becomes\n\\[\n8^{10}+4^{10}=2^{30}+2^{20}\n=2^{20}(2^{10}+1),\n\\]\nand the denominator becomes\n\\[\n8^4+4^{11}=2^{12}+2^{22}\n=2^{12}(1+2^{10}).\n\\]\nThe parenthesized factors are equal and positive, so they can be canceled. Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{8^{10}+4^{10}}{8^4+4^{11}}\n=\\frac{2^{20}}{2^{12}}=2^8=256.\n\\]\nThe radical denotes the nonnegative square root, giving \\(\\sqrt{256}=\\boxed{16}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
